From 2823bc96eff4423a8b07b71ce4369447b4a3b50d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:08:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] gpio: add Alp Lab GD32 bridge expander driver

Copyright (c) 2026 Alp Lab AB
SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0

New driver for the GPIO expander provided by the GD32G553 IO-MCU on
Alp Lab V2N-family SoMs (E1M V2N101/V2N102). The MCU acts as a
supervisor bridge between the RZ/V2N SoC and a set of E1M IO pads;
its I2C slave (BRD_I2C @ 0x70, Linux i2c8) is the transport used here.

Wire format:
  - Write: [reg 0x00][cmd][payload][CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE lo][hi]
  - Read:  [status][payload][CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE lo][hi] (no SOF on I2C)
  - The slave clock-stretches until the reply is ready.

Sticky-output caveat: CMD_GPIO_WRITE promotes a pad to push-pull
output permanently (until GD32 reset). direction_input() returns
-EPERM on a line that has ever been written; this matches the
bridge firmware hardware model.

Bridge-liveness handling: the GD32 may be unflashed, held in reset, or
its BRD_I2C bus wedged at boot. probe() runs a best-effort GET_VERSION
handshake (result logged) but registers the gpiochip unconditionally.
Returning -EPROBE_DEFER here would also defer every consumer of these
lines -- notably the Display-1 panel reset -- holding the entire DSI/DU
pipeline hostage to the bridge's liveness. Individual GPIO ops instead
fail gracefully (dev_warn) and begin working once the bridge answers.

Lines 18/19 (bt-reg-on/wl-reg-on): the bridge pad map grows two more
lines at firmware 0.2.12 (PROTOCOL_VERSION_MINOR 11) for the on-module
Murata LBEE5HY2FY-922 Wi-Fi+BT REG_ON pair. The chip always registers
all 20 lines; lines 18/19 are gated per-consumer in a .request()
callback that runs one non-blocking GET_VERSION attempt on demand and
caches the result -- 0 once a confirmed minor >= 11 (major 0), -ENODEV
once confirmed below that or on an unexpected major, -EPROBE_DEFER
while the bridge is still silent.

Late-bridge deferral (Refs #2297; bug bench-observed 2026-09-26,
E1M-V2M103, this fix compile-verified only so far -- not yet re-run on
the bench): a bare -EPROBE_DEFER from .request() only gets retried by
the kernel when some OTHER driver's (un)registration happens to walk
the deferred-probe list -- nothing guarantees that happens once the
bridge later comes up. Observed on the bench: NRST release failed, and
/sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred listed 15c20000.mmc, wlan-pwrseq and
serial0-0 for the whole boot even though the bridge came up seconds
later. .request() must still return -EPROBE_DEFER rather than grant
the request while unresolved, though: mmc-pwrseq-simple and hci_bcm
each run a one-shot power-up sequence (mmc_rescan() / loading the .hcd
patch) immediately once .request() succeeds, and SDHI2 being
non-removable means that sequence never reruns on its own if it runs
before REG_ON can actually move.

What resolves the deferral instead: a new, independent delayed_work,
gd32_bridge_resolve_work(), polls GET_VERSION regardless of whether any
consumer has requested the lines yet. On resolving either way (0
confirmed supported, or -ENODEV confirmed unsupported) it briefly
registers a throwaway platform_device (see
gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe()) purely so that device's bind runs
driver_bound() -> the kernel's own driver_deferred_probe_trigger()
(drivers/base/dd.c, confirmed present at that call site in this kernel
tree) -- the only in-tree hook that lets module code queue a
deferred-probe retry (asynchronous, on system_unbound_wq) on demand.
That gets mmc-pwrseq-simple / hci_bcm to retry .request() with the real
answer -- granted, or failing outright with -ENODEV -- instead of
lingering deferred indefinitely.

Output-state replay (Refs #2297): a write attempted while the bridge is
unreachable (bench-proven: "GPIO write line 5 failed" against the
Display-1 panel reset-gpios consumer at probe, before the GD32 has come
up) used to be forgotten forever, and a GD32 reset (WDT, OTA A/B swap)
silently drops every pad back to its boot default -- notably the Wi-Fi
REG_ON lines (18/19), which then need re-asserting. Fixed by (1)
latching output_mask/output_vals under a new state_lock before
attempting the write and holding that lock for the transfer's entire
duration (not just the latch), so a concurrent replay can never read a
stale snapshot and land its write after a newer one, and (2) a
delayed_work that re-issues one idempotent CMD_GPIO_WRITE(output_mask,
output_vals) about once a second whenever output_mask != 0 -- glitch-free
on pads already at that level, so no reset detection is needed; costs
one small I2C frame/s on BRD_I2C (i2c8). Because the requested state is
always latched and eventually replayed, direction_output() always
reports success instead of propagating the transfer error: its
consumers here (the Display-1 panel reset-gpios, mmc-pwrseq-simple, a
Bluetooth shutdown-gpios) are ordinary gpiod_direction_output() callers,
not OF gpio-hogs, and have no -EPROBE_DEFER equivalent once past
.request() -- a nonzero return would fail the *consumer's* own probe
permanently over what is usually only a transient bridge-down window,
instead of the consumer picking up the correct state on the next replay.

Note: only the Linux gpio-gd32-bridge driver re-asserts. The CM33/Zephyr
side of this bridge does not yet, so #2297 stays open for that half.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Alp Lab SoM-specific supervisor MCU protocol]

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
 drivers/gpio/Kconfig            |   9 +
 drivers/gpio/Makefile           |   1 +
 drivers/gpio/gpio-gd32-bridge.c | 845 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 855 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/gpio/gpio-gd32-bridge.c

diff --git a/drivers/gpio/Kconfig b/drivers/gpio/Kconfig
index 74d8b949c..eefc447b2 100644
--- a/drivers/gpio/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpio/Kconfig
@@ -1009,6 +1009,15 @@ config GPIO_ADNP
 	  enough to represent all pins, but the driver will assume a
 	  register layout for 64 pins (8 registers).
 
+config GPIO_GD32_BRIDGE
+	tristate "Alp Lab GD32 IO-MCU bridge GPIO expander"
+	depends on I2C && OF
+	select CRC_ITU_T
+	help
+	  GPIO support for E1M IO pads routed through the GD32G553
+	  supervisor MCU on Alp Lab V2N-family SoMs, accessed over the
+	  bridge's I2C slave interface.
+
 config GPIO_GW_PLD
 	tristate "Gateworks PLD GPIO Expander"
 	depends on OF_GPIO
diff --git a/drivers/gpio/Makefile b/drivers/gpio/Makefile
index e331e951e..3ba5cc2f2 100644
--- a/drivers/gpio/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/gpio/Makefile
@@ -59,6 +59,7 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_EP93XX)		+= gpio-ep93xx.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_EXAR)			+= gpio-exar.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_F7188X)		+= gpio-f7188x.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_FTGPIO010)		+= gpio-ftgpio010.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_GD32_BRIDGE)		+= gpio-gd32-bridge.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_GE_FPGA)		+= gpio-ge.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_GPIO_MM)		+= gpio-gpio-mm.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_GPIO_GRGPIO)		+= gpio-grgpio.o
diff --git a/drivers/gpio/gpio-gd32-bridge.c b/drivers/gpio/gpio-gd32-bridge.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b202babc2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/gpio/gpio-gd32-bridge.c
@@ -0,0 +1,845 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * GPIO driver for the Alp Lab GD32 IO-MCU bridge (I2C transport).
+ *
+ * The E1M V2N-family SoM routes a set of E1M IO pads through a GD32G553
+ * supervisor MCU. This driver exposes the bridge's logical GPIO map as a
+ * Linux gpiochip over the bridge's I2C slave (BRD_I2C @ 0x70) using the
+ * bridge wire protocol CMD_GPIO_READ/CMD_GPIO_WRITE.
+ *
+ * Wire notes:
+ *  - write:  [reg 0x00][cmd][payload][crc16 lo][crc16 hi]
+ *  - read:   [status][payload][crc16 lo][crc16 hi]   (no SOF on I2C)
+ *  - CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE over cmd|payload (write) / status|payload (read).
+ *  - Reply length is opcode-derived; the slave clock-stretches until ready.
+ *  - GPIO_WRITE auto-promotes a pad to push-pull output, sticky until the
+ *    GD32 resets; there is no demote-to-input. direction_input() is
+ *    therefore only honoured for never-written lines.
+ *
+ * Lines 18/19 (bt-reg-on / wl-reg-on): the Murata LBEE5HY2FY-922
+ * (Infineon CYW55513) Wi-Fi+BT module's two REG_ON enables, added to
+ * the bridge pad map at firmware 0.2.12 / PROTOCOL_VERSION_MINOR 11
+ * (pad count 18 -> 20; GD32 boots them OUTPUT LOW).
+ *
+ * The chip always registers all 20 lines now: a fixed line count
+ * decided from a single best-effort GET_VERSION at probe() bakes in
+ * whatever the bridge's boot race happened to answer at that instant
+ * (~1.5 s in, this consistently reads "bridge not answering (-6)" --
+ * the GD32 is not up yet -- pinning the chip at 18 lines forever, even
+ * once the bridge comes up seconds later). Instead, lines 18/19 are
+ * gated at .request() time: the first consumer to request either line
+ * runs one non-blocking GET_VERSION attempt (gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt())
+ * and caches the result. A confirmed minor < GD32_PROTO_MINOR_WIFI_BT (or
+ * an unexpected protocol major) is cached as -ENODEV: that firmware never
+ * learned these two pads, and letting a consumer issue GPIO_WRITE against
+ * pad bits it silently ignores would power nothing while reporting
+ * success. .request() fails with -ENODEV in that definitive case, and
+ * with -EPROBE_DEFER while the bridge is still silent.
+ *
+ * Bug (Refs #2297, bench-observed 2026-09-26, E1M-V2M103): a bare
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER from .request() only gets retried by the kernel when some
+ * OTHER driver's (un)registration happens to walk the deferred-probe
+ * list, which nothing guarantees will happen once the bridge later comes
+ * up: mmc-pwrseq-simple's wlan-pwrseq reset-gpios and hci_bcm's shutdown-
+ * gpios (lines 19/18) sat in /sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred for the
+ * whole boot, alongside the mmc host itself, even though the bridge came
+ * up seconds later. .request() must still return -EPROBE_DEFER rather
+ * than grant the request while unresolved, though: mmc-pwrseq-simple and
+ * hci_bcm each run a one-shot power-up sequence (mmc_rescan() / loading
+ * the .hcd patch) immediately once .request() succeeds, and SDHI2 being
+ * non-removable means that sequence never reruns on its own if it runs
+ * before REG_ON can actually move.
+ *
+ * What resolves the deferral instead: a dedicated
+ * gd32_bridge_resolve_work(), independent of any consumer request or the
+ * output-state replay below, polls GET_VERSION regardless of whether
+ * anyone has requested the lines yet. On resolving either way (0
+ * confirmed supported, or -ENODEV confirmed unsupported) it briefly
+ * registers a throwaway platform_device (see
+ * gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe()) purely so that device's bind runs
+ * driver_bound() -> the kernel's own driver_deferred_probe_trigger()
+ * (drivers/base/dd.c) -- the only in-tree hook that lets module code
+ * queue a deferred-probe retry (asynchronous, on system_unbound_wq) on
+ * demand. That is what gets mmc-pwrseq-simple / hci_bcm to retry
+ * .request() with the real answer -- granted, or failing outright with
+ * -ENODEV -- instead of lingering deferred indefinitely.
+ *
+ * Output-state replay: a write attempted while the bridge is unreachable
+ * (at probe, or after any GD32 reset -- WDT, OTA A/B swap) is never lost,
+ * and a GD32 reset also drops every pad back to its boot default -- see
+ * issue #2297: the Wi-Fi REG_ON lines need re-asserting after a GD32
+ * reset. Handled two ways: (1) output_mask/output_vals are latched under
+ * state_lock BEFORE the write is attempted and for its entire duration
+ * (see the struct comment on the lock order), so the requested state
+ * always survives a failed transfer and a concurrent replay cannot land a
+ * stale write after a newer one; (2) a delayed_work re-issues one
+ * idempotent CMD_GPIO_WRITE(output_mask, output_vals) roughly once a
+ * second whenever output_mask != 0 -- glitch-free on pads already at that
+ * level, so it needs no reset detection, just a period short enough that
+ * a freshly-up bridge is re-promoted quickly. Costs one small I2C
+ * frame/s on BRD_I2C (i2c8) while any line is held as output.
+ *
+ * Consequently direction_output() always reports success rather than
+ * propagating the transfer error: its consumers (the Display-1 panel
+ * reset-gpios, mmc-pwrseq-simple, a Bluetooth shutdown-gpios) are
+ * ordinary gpiod_direction_output() callers with no equivalent of
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER once past .request() -- a nonzero return here fails the
+ * *consumer's* probe permanently over what is usually only a transient
+ * bridge-down window, with no automatic retry. Swallowing it instead lets
+ * the consumer's own probe proceed; the requested state is latched
+ * regardless and gd32_bridge_replay_work() applies it once the bridge
+ * answers, so the consumer just cannot tell the line is actually driven
+ * until the next successful replay.
+ *
+ * Copyright (c) 2026 Alp Lab AB
+ */
+
+#include <asm/unaligned.h>
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/crc-itu-t.h>
+#include <linux/gpio/driver.h>
+#include <linux/i2c.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
+
+#define GD32_REG_CMD		0x00
+#define GD32_CMD_PING		0x00
+#define GD32_CMD_GET_VERSION	0x01
+#define GD32_CMD_GPIO_READ	0x10
+#define GD32_CMD_GPIO_WRITE	0x11
+#define GD32_STATUS_OK		0x00
+
+#define GD32_NGPIO_BASE		18
+#define GD32_NGPIO_WIFI_BT	20
+
+/* Bridge protocol minor at which lines 18/19 (bt-reg-on/wl-reg-on) first
+ * exist. A bridge below this minor has no notion of them. */
+#define GD32_PROTO_MINOR_WIFI_BT	11
+
+/*
+ * gd32_bridge_resolve_work() poll period (Refs #2297): independent of, and
+ * much less urgent than, the output-state replay below -- this just needs
+ * to notice a slow bridge coming up sometime during boot, not glitch-free
+ * pad re-promotion.
+ *
+ * Flat at GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS (1 Hz) for the first
+ * GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_FLAT_ATTEMPTS ticks (~30 s, comfortably past every
+ * bridge boot time observed so far), then backs off exponentially up to
+ * the GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_CAP_MS cap. Never gives up outright: the
+ * bridge firmware can be reset or OTA'd at any point during a long-running
+ * boot, and the whole point of this poller is to notice that eventually
+ * rather than depend on some unrelated event to re-probe.
+ */
+#define GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS		1000
+#define GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_FLAT_ATTEMPTS		30
+#define GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_CAP_MS		30000
+
+/* Name (and platform_driver.driver.name) of the throwaway platform_device
+ * gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe() registers purely to retrigger deferred
+ * probing once lines 18/19 resolve, either way -- see the file-header
+ * comment and gd32_bridge_kick_probe(). */
+#define GD32_DEFERRED_KICK_DEVNAME	"gd32-bridge-deferred-kick"
+
+/* Bit mask covering lines 18/19 only, for masking them out of a replay
+ * when the bridge has not (or not yet) confirmed it understands them. */
+#define GD32_WIFI_BT_LINE_MASK	GENMASK(GD32_NGPIO_WIFI_BT - 1, GD32_NGPIO_BASE)
+
+/* Output-state replay period: frequent enough that a bridge which just
+ * came up (post-reset or first power-on) is re-promoted within about a
+ * second, cheap enough (one small I2C frame) to run indefinitely on
+ * BRD_I2C (i2c8) whenever any line is held as output. */
+#define GD32_REPLAY_PERIOD_MS	1000
+
+static const char * const gd32_bridge_gpio_names[GD32_NGPIO_WIFI_BT] = {
+	"IO8",  "IO9",  "IO10", "IO11", "IO12", "IO13",
+	"IO14", "IO16", "IO24", "IO25", "IO27", "IO28",
+	"IO29", "IO30", "IO31", "IO32", "IO34", "IO35",
+	"bt-reg-on", "wl-reg-on",
+};
+
+struct gd32_bridge_gpio {
+	struct i2c_client *client;
+	struct gpio_chip chip;
+	struct mutex lock;		/* serialize cmd/reply pairs */
+	/*
+	 * output_mask: lines promoted to output (sticky; once written a pad
+	 * stays push-pull until GD32 reset). Bits are only ever added, never
+	 * cleared.
+	 * output_vals: last requested levels. Latched even when the bridge
+	 * rejects or fails the write -- gd32_bridge_replay_work() keeps
+	 * retrying until it lands.
+	 *
+	 * Guarded by state_lock, held across the *entire* transfer (not just
+	 * the read-modify-write of these two fields) in both
+	 * gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set() and gd32_bridge_replay_work(): gpiolib
+	 * only serializes per-line callback invocations against each other,
+	 * not against this driver's own workqueue, so without state_lock
+	 * spanning the transfer a replay reading a stale snapshot could land
+	 * its write after a newer .set()/direction_output() call and drive a
+	 * line back to an earlier level.
+	 *
+	 * Lock order (always this direction, never reversed):
+	 * state_lock -> status_lock -> gd32_bridge_xfer()'s own `lock`.
+	 * gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status() (status_lock only) and
+	 * gd32_bridge_xfer() (`lock` only) are both called while state_lock
+	 * is held; can_sleep is true, so blocking I2C under a mutex is fine.
+	 */
+	u32 output_mask;
+	u32 output_vals;
+	struct mutex state_lock;	/* protects output_mask/output_vals; see lock order above */
+	struct delayed_work replay_work;
+	bool replay_failing;		/* guarded by state_lock; logs transitions once */
+	/*
+	 * Lines 18/19 (bt-reg-on/wl-reg-on) gating, resolved lazily at
+	 * .request() time rather than once at probe() -- see the
+	 * file-header comment. wifi_bt_reg_on_status caches the resolved
+	 * errno: 0 (supported), -ENODEV (confirmed unsupported), or
+	 * -EPROBE_DEFER (not yet resolved -- retried by .request() itself
+	 * with one non-blocking attempt each time, and independently by
+	 * gd32_bridge_resolve_work() regardless of whether anyone has
+	 * requested the lines).
+	 * Guarded by status_lock, a lock separate from the cmd/reply lock
+	 * above: gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt() reads/writes this field
+	 * around calls into gd32_bridge_xfer(), which takes `lock` itself
+	 * -- reusing one mutex for both would self-deadlock.
+	 */
+	int wifi_bt_reg_on_status;
+	struct mutex status_lock;
+	/*
+	 * Independent lines-18/19 resolver, NOT under state_lock (it has
+	 * nothing to do with output_mask/output_vals or the replay above) --
+	 * see gd32_bridge_resolve_work() and the file-header comment.
+	 * resolve_attempts is touched only from gd32_bridge_resolve_work()
+	 * itself (a single delayed_work instance never runs concurrently
+	 * with itself), so it needs no lock of its own.
+	 */
+	struct delayed_work resolve_work;
+	unsigned int resolve_attempts;
+};
+
+/* Peek the cached lines-18/19 status without triggering a new bridge
+ * round-trip (used from write paths, where re-polling would be
+ * surprising). Use gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt() to actually resolve it. */
+static int gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32)
+{
+	int status;
+
+	mutex_lock(&gd32->status_lock);
+	status = gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status;
+	mutex_unlock(&gd32->status_lock);
+
+	return status;
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_xfer(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32, u8 cmd,
+			    const u8 *req, unsigned int req_len,
+			    u8 *rsp, unsigned int rsp_len)
+{
+	struct i2c_client *cl = gd32->client;
+	u8 wbuf[2 + 8 + 2];	/* reg + cmd + max payload + crc */
+	u8 rbuf[1 + 4 + 2];	/* status + max payload + crc */
+	unsigned int wlen = 2 + req_len + 2;
+	unsigned int rlen = 1 + rsp_len + 2;
+	struct i2c_msg msgs[2];
+	u16 crc;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (WARN_ON(req_len > 8 || rsp_len > 4))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	wbuf[0] = GD32_REG_CMD;
+	wbuf[1] = cmd;
+	memcpy(&wbuf[2], req, req_len);
+	crc = crc_itu_t(0xFFFF, &wbuf[1], 1 + req_len);
+	wbuf[2 + req_len] = crc & 0xFF;
+	wbuf[3 + req_len] = crc >> 8;
+
+	msgs[0].addr  = cl->addr;
+	msgs[0].flags = 0;
+	msgs[0].len   = wlen;
+	msgs[0].buf   = wbuf;
+	msgs[1].addr  = cl->addr;
+	msgs[1].flags = I2C_M_RD;
+	msgs[1].len   = rlen;
+	msgs[1].buf   = rbuf;
+
+	mutex_lock(&gd32->lock);
+	ret = i2c_transfer(cl->adapter, msgs, 2);
+	mutex_unlock(&gd32->lock);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	if (ret != 2)
+		return -EIO;
+
+	crc = crc_itu_t(0xFFFF, rbuf, 1 + rsp_len);
+	if (rbuf[1 + rsp_len] != (crc & 0xFF) ||
+	    rbuf[2 + rsp_len] != (crc >> 8))
+		return -EBADMSG;
+	if (rbuf[0] != GD32_STATUS_OK)
+		return -EIO;
+	if (rsp_len)
+		memcpy(rsp, &rbuf[1], rsp_len);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_get(struct gpio_chip *chip, unsigned int offset)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+	u8 req[4], rsp[4];
+	u32 mask = BIT(offset), levels;
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Defensive: .request() already refuses lines 18/19 until
+	 * gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt() confirms support, so an in-kernel
+	 * consumer reaching .get() here without a granted request should not
+	 * happen. Gate anyway rather than reading bits the firmware may not
+	 * implement.
+	 */
+	if ((mask & GD32_WIFI_BT_LINE_MASK) && gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(gd32) != 0)
+		return -EAGAIN;
+
+	put_unaligned_le32(mask, req);
+	ret = gd32_bridge_xfer(gd32, GD32_CMD_GPIO_READ, req, 4, rsp, 4);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	levels = get_unaligned_le32(rsp);
+	return !!(levels & mask);
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32,
+				   unsigned int offset, int value)
+{
+	u8 req[8];
+	u32 mask = BIT(offset);
+	bool is_wifi_bt = mask & GD32_WIFI_BT_LINE_MASK;
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Latch the requested state and hold state_lock across the transfer
+	 * itself -- see the struct comment's lock-order note. This
+	 * serializes against gd32_bridge_replay_work(): without it, a
+	 * replay could read a stale snapshot and land its write after this
+	 * one, driving the line back to an earlier level.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&gd32->state_lock);
+	gd32->output_mask |= mask;
+	if (value)
+		gd32->output_vals |= mask;
+	else
+		gd32->output_vals &= ~mask;
+
+	/*
+	 * Defensive only: .request() refuses lines 18/19 until support is
+	 * confirmed (gd32_bridge_gpio_request()), so do_set() should never
+	 * actually reach this with is_wifi_bt true and status != 0. Skip the
+	 * wire transfer for those bits anyway rather than assume that can
+	 * never change -- the level is still latched above regardless, and
+	 * gd32_bridge_replay_work() would re-issue the write if support were
+	 * ever (re)confirmed, so nothing would be lost even if this were hit.
+	 */
+	if (!is_wifi_bt || gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(gd32) == 0) {
+		put_unaligned_le32(mask, &req[0]);
+		put_unaligned_le32(value ? mask : 0, &req[4]);
+		ret = gd32_bridge_xfer(gd32, GD32_CMD_GPIO_WRITE, req, 8, NULL, 0);
+		if (ret)
+			gd32->replay_failing = true;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Make sure the periodic replay is running: it is not scheduled
+	 * while output_mask is 0 (nothing to replay), so the first write of
+	 * any line needs to (re)kick it. Idempotent if already queued.
+	 */
+	queue_delayed_work(system_freezable_power_efficient_wq,
+			   &gd32->replay_work,
+			   msecs_to_jiffies(GD32_REPLAY_PERIOD_MS));
+	mutex_unlock(&gd32->state_lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static void gd32_bridge_gpio_set(struct gpio_chip *chip, unsigned int offset,
+				 int value)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+	int ret = gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set(gd32, offset, value);
+
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(chip->parent,
+				     "GPIO write line %u failed (%d)\n",
+				     offset, ret);
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_dir_out(struct gpio_chip *chip,
+				    unsigned int offset, int value)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+	int ret = gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set(gd32, offset, value);
+
+	/*
+	 * Swallow the transfer error rather than propagate it. This chip's
+	 * consumers (the Display-1 panel reset-gpios, mmc-pwrseq-simple, a
+	 * Bluetooth shutdown-gpios) are ordinary gpiod_direction_output()
+	 * callers, not OF gpio-hogs -- a nonzero return here fails the
+	 * *consumer's* own probe permanently (there is no -EPROBE_DEFER
+	 * equivalent once past .request()), over what is usually only a
+	 * transient bridge-down window with no automatic retry. The
+	 * requested state is already latched (see
+	 * gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set()) and gd32_bridge_replay_work() applies
+	 * it once the bridge answers, so returning 0 unconditionally lets
+	 * the consumer's probe proceed; it just cannot yet tell the line is
+	 * actually driven until the next successful replay.
+	 */
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(chip->parent,
+				     "GPIO write line %u failed (%d)\n",
+				     offset, ret);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_dir_in(struct gpio_chip *chip, unsigned int offset)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+
+	/*
+	 * Sticky promotion: once written, a pad cannot return to input.
+	 * READ_ONCE, not state_lock: output_mask bits are only ever added
+	 * (see the struct comment), so an unlocked peek can only be stale
+	 * in the safe direction (a just-promoted pad briefly still reading
+	 * as input), never the other way.
+	 */
+	if (READ_ONCE(gd32->output_mask) & BIT(offset))
+		return -EPERM;
+	return 0;	/* input; pull behaviour depends on bridge firmware version */
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_get_dir(struct gpio_chip *chip,
+				    unsigned int offset)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+
+	return (READ_ONCE(gd32->output_mask) & BIT(offset)) ? GPIO_LINE_DIRECTION_OUT
+							     : GPIO_LINE_DIRECTION_IN;
+}
+
+/*
+ * One non-blocking GET_VERSION attempt against the bridge, updating the
+ * cached lines-18/19 status on success. Never sleeps or retries -- a
+ * caller that needs a bounded wait loops this via
+ * gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt() instead; gd32_bridge_resolve_work() calls
+ * this directly, once per tick, independently of any consumer request.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 and updates the cache on a successful GET_VERSION, or the
+ * xfer error (typically -ETIMEDOUT/-EIO) with the cache left untouched
+ * (still -EPROBE_DEFER) when the bridge stays silent.
+ */
+static int gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_poll_once(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32)
+{
+	u8 ver[3];
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = gd32_bridge_xfer(gd32, GD32_CMD_GET_VERSION, NULL, 0, ver, 3);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&gd32->status_lock);
+	/*
+	 * major 0 is the only protocol generation this driver understands;
+	 * a nonzero major is a wire-format change we cannot assume is
+	 * backward compatible, so treat it the same as "unsupported"
+	 * rather than trusting the minor field under different semantics.
+	 */
+	if (ver[0] != 0 || ver[1] < GD32_PROTO_MINOR_WIFI_BT) {
+		if (gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status != -ENODEV)
+			dev_warn(gd32->chip.parent,
+				 "bridge protocol %u.%u.%u has no bt-reg-on/wl-reg-on lines (need major 0, minor >= %u); lines 18/19 (bt-reg-on/wl-reg-on) will never be driven on this firmware\n",
+				 ver[0], ver[1], ver[2], GD32_PROTO_MINOR_WIFI_BT);
+		gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status = -ENODEV;
+	} else {
+		gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status = 0;
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&gd32->status_lock);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Resolve (and cache) whether this bridge exposes lines 18/19. Called
+ * from .request() -- see the file-header comment.
+ *
+ * One non-blocking GET_VERSION attempt only: a bridge that is already up
+ * resolves immediately, and a bridge that is merely slow to boot is
+ * gd32_bridge_resolve_work()'s job, not this function's -- blocking
+ * .request() itself to wait it out would only delay that consumer's own
+ * probe for no benefit now that the independent poller exists.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 if confirmed supported, -ENODEV if confirmed unsupported, or
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER if the bridge is still silent.
+ */
+static int gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32)
+{
+	int cached = gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(gd32);
+
+	if (cached != -EPROBE_DEFER)
+		return cached;
+
+	if (gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_poll_once(gd32))
+		return -EPROBE_DEFER;	/* still silent */
+
+	return gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(gd32);
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_request(struct gpio_chip *chip,
+				    unsigned int offset)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+
+	if (offset < GD32_NGPIO_BASE)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * -EPROBE_DEFER here defers the *consumer's* own probe
+	 * (mmc-pwrseq-simple's wlan-pwrseq reset-gpios, hci_bcm's
+	 * shutdown-gpios) -- deliberate: each runs a one-shot power-up
+	 * sequence right after .request() succeeds, and SDHI2 being
+	 * non-removable means that sequence never reruns on its own if it
+	 * runs before REG_ON can actually move, so granting the request
+	 * while still unresolved is not safe (Refs #2297). See the
+	 * file-header comment: gd32_bridge_resolve_work() discovers a slow
+	 * bridge independently and retriggers deferred probing once
+	 * resolved either way, so this consumer gets called again with the
+	 * real answer.
+	 */
+	return gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt(gd32);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Periodically re-assert output_mask/output_vals against the bridge. A
+ * GD32 reset (WDT, OTA A/B swap) silently drops every pad back to its
+ * boot default, and a write attempted while the bridge was unreachable
+ * (e.g. at probe, before the GD32 has come up) is latched but not yet
+ * applied -- see gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set(). Re-issuing CMD_GPIO_WRITE for
+ * the whole mask is glitch-free on pads already at that level, so this
+ * needs no reset detection: it just needs to run often enough that a
+ * freshly-up bridge is re-promoted within about one period. Reschedules
+ * itself only while output_mask != 0; gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set() (re)kicks
+ * it whenever a line is written.
+ */
+static void gd32_bridge_replay_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
+						     struct gd32_bridge_gpio,
+						     replay_work);
+	u8 req[8];
+	u32 mask, vals, xfer_mask;
+	int ret;
+	bool active;
+
+	/*
+	 * Hold state_lock across the transfer itself, not just the
+	 * snapshot -- see the struct comment's lock-order note. Without
+	 * this, a concurrent gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set() could land a newer
+	 * write in between this function's read and its own transfer,
+	 * and this stale replay would then drive the line back down.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&gd32->state_lock);
+	mask = gd32->output_mask;
+	vals = gd32->output_vals;
+
+	/*
+	 * Lines 18/19 only ever enter output_mask once .request() has
+	 * resolved them supported (gd32_bridge_gpio_request() now refuses
+	 * the request otherwise, see the file-header comment), so this is
+	 * defensive rather than a live path: mask them out unless confirmed
+	 * supported, so a replay never sends bits the firmware may not
+	 * understand. Resolving a still-unresolved bridge is
+	 * gd32_bridge_resolve_work()'s job, independent of this replay and
+	 * NOT run under state_lock.
+	 */
+	xfer_mask = mask;
+	if (gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_status(gd32) != 0)
+		xfer_mask &= ~GD32_WIFI_BT_LINE_MASK;
+
+	if (xfer_mask) {
+		put_unaligned_le32(xfer_mask, &req[0]);
+		put_unaligned_le32(vals & xfer_mask, &req[4]);
+		ret = gd32_bridge_xfer(gd32, GD32_CMD_GPIO_WRITE, req, 8,
+				       NULL, 0);
+		if (ret) {
+			if (!gd32->replay_failing) {
+				dev_warn(gd32->chip.parent,
+					 "output state replay failed (%d)\n",
+					 ret);
+				gd32->replay_failing = true;
+			}
+		} else if (gd32->replay_failing) {
+			dev_info(gd32->chip.parent,
+				 "bridge reachable, %u line(s) re-applied\n",
+				 hweight32(xfer_mask));
+			gd32->replay_failing = false;
+		}
+	}
+	active = mask != 0;
+	mutex_unlock(&gd32->state_lock);
+
+	if (active)
+		queue_delayed_work(system_freezable_power_efficient_wq,
+				   &gd32->replay_work,
+				   msecs_to_jiffies(GD32_REPLAY_PERIOD_MS));
+}
+
+static void gd32_bridge_cancel_replay(void *data)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = data;
+
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&gd32->replay_work);
+}
+
+/*
+ * No-op probe: this device exists purely so registering it BINDS. Binding
+ * runs driver_bound(), which (drivers/base/dd.c in this tree, verified on
+ * the actual E1M-V2M103 kernel source) unconditionally calls
+ * driver_deferred_probe_trigger() -- see gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe().
+ */
+static int gd32_bridge_kick_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static struct platform_driver gd32_bridge_kick_driver = {
+	.driver = {
+		.name = GD32_DEFERRED_KICK_DEVNAME,
+	},
+	.probe = gd32_bridge_kick_probe,
+};
+
+/*
+ * Fire on lines 18/19 resolving either way (0 confirmed supported, -ENODEV
+ * confirmed unsupported -- see gd32_bridge_resolve_work()). Registers a
+ * throwaway platform_device that matches gd32_bridge_kick_driver by name;
+ * its probe() always succeeds, so platform_device_register_simple() drives
+ * it synchronously through bus_probe_device() -> ... -> driver_bound(),
+ * which (see the comment on gd32_bridge_kick_probe()) unconditionally
+ * calls driver_deferred_probe_trigger() -- that queues a deferred-probe
+ * retry (asynchronous, on system_unbound_wq) for every currently-deferred
+ * device, including mmc-pwrseq-simple's wlan-pwrseq and hci_bcm's
+ * shutdown-gpios, which get called again with the real answer: granted, or
+ * failing outright with -ENODEV instead of lingering. The device has done
+ * its job the moment it binds, so it is unregistered again immediately;
+ * PLATFORM_DEVID_AUTO avoids any name collision if more than one bridge
+ * instance is ever present on one system.
+ */
+static void gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe(struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32)
+{
+	struct platform_device *pdev;
+
+	pdev = platform_device_register_simple(GD32_DEFERRED_KICK_DEVNAME,
+						PLATFORM_DEVID_AUTO, NULL, 0);
+	if (IS_ERR(pdev)) {
+		dev_warn(gd32->chip.parent,
+			 "failed to register %s (%ld); deferred consumers of lines 18/19 will only pick this up on their own next retry\n",
+			 GD32_DEFERRED_KICK_DEVNAME, PTR_ERR(pdev));
+		return;
+	}
+
+	platform_device_unregister(pdev);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Independent lazy resolver for lines 18/19 (Refs #2297; see the
+ * file-header comment for why this exists separately from both
+ * .request() and gd32_bridge_replay_work()). Polls GET_VERSION, NOT under
+ * state_lock -- this has nothing to do with output_mask/output_vals --
+ * regardless of whether any consumer has requested the lines yet, so a
+ * bridge that is merely slow to boot still gets discovered even after the
+ * first .request() has already given up and returned -EPROBE_DEFER to its
+ * caller.
+ */
+static void gd32_bridge_resolve_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
+						     struct gd32_bridge_gpio,
+						     resolve_work);
+	unsigned int shift, period_ms;
+
+	if (!gd32_bridge_wifi_bt_poll_once(gd32)) {
+		/*
+		 * Resolved either way -- kick deferred probing once so
+		 * mmc-pwrseq-simple / hci_bcm get a deterministic answer now
+		 * instead of lingering (see gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe()).
+		 * Terminal: do not reschedule.
+		 */
+		gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe(gd32);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/* Still silent -- see GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS's comment for the
+	 * flat-then-backoff schedule. Clamp the shift itself (not just the
+	 * result) to avoid an oversized shift count as resolve_attempts
+	 * grows unbounded over a very long uptime. */
+	gd32->resolve_attempts++;
+	if (gd32->resolve_attempts <= GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_FLAT_ATTEMPTS) {
+		period_ms = GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS;
+	} else {
+		shift = gd32->resolve_attempts - GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_FLAT_ATTEMPTS;
+		if (shift > 5)
+			shift = 5;	/* 1000 << 5 = 32000 already past the cap */
+		period_ms = min_t(unsigned int, GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS << shift,
+				   GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_CAP_MS);
+	}
+
+	queue_delayed_work(system_freezable_power_efficient_wq,
+			   &gd32->resolve_work, msecs_to_jiffies(period_ms));
+}
+
+static void gd32_bridge_cancel_resolve(void *data)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32 = data;
+
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&gd32->resolve_work);
+}
+
+static int gd32_bridge_gpio_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	struct gd32_bridge_gpio *gd32;
+	u8 ver[3];
+	int ret;
+
+	gd32 = devm_kzalloc(&client->dev, sizeof(*gd32), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!gd32)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	gd32->client = client;
+	mutex_init(&gd32->lock);
+	mutex_init(&gd32->status_lock);
+	mutex_init(&gd32->state_lock);
+	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&gd32->replay_work, gd32_bridge_replay_work);
+	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&gd32->resolve_work, gd32_bridge_resolve_work);
+	gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status = -EPROBE_DEFER;	/* not yet resolved */
+
+	gd32->chip.label	  = "gd32-bridge-gpio";
+	gd32->chip.parent	  = &client->dev;
+	gd32->chip.owner	  = THIS_MODULE;
+	gd32->chip.base		  = -1;
+
+	/*
+	 * Best-effort handshake: log the bridge protocol if it answers, but
+	 * register the gpiochip regardless of the result.  The GD32 may be
+	 * unflashed, held in reset, or its BRD_I2C bus wedged at boot; a
+	 * -EPROBE_DEFER here would also defer every consumer of these lines
+	 * -- notably the Display-1 panel reset -- holding the whole DSI/DU
+	 * pipeline hostage to the bridge's liveness.  Expose the gpiochip
+	 * immediately instead; individual GPIO ops fail gracefully (see
+	 * gd32_bridge_gpio_get/_set) and start working once the bridge is up.
+	 *
+	 * The chip always registers all GD32_NGPIO_WIFI_BT lines -- lines
+	 * 18/19 are gated per-consumer in .request(), not by this probe-time
+	 * handshake (see the file-header comment and
+	 * gd32_bridge_resolve_wifi_bt()). If this handshake happens to land
+	 * (the bridge was already up), seed the cache now so the first
+	 * consumer of lines 18/19 doesn't pay for a second round-trip.
+	 */
+	ret = gd32_bridge_xfer(gd32, GD32_CMD_GET_VERSION, NULL, 0, ver, 3);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(&client->dev,
+			 "bridge not answering (%d); registering gpiochip anyway -- GPIO ops will error until it is up\n",
+			 ret);
+	} else {
+		dev_info(&client->dev, "GD32 bridge protocol %u.%u.%u\n",
+			 ver[0], ver[1], ver[2]);
+		if (ver[0] != 0 || ver[1] < GD32_PROTO_MINOR_WIFI_BT)
+			gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status = -ENODEV;
+		else
+			gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status = 0;
+	}
+
+	gd32->chip.ngpio	  = GD32_NGPIO_WIFI_BT;
+	gd32->chip.names	  = gd32_bridge_gpio_names;
+	gd32->chip.request	  = gd32_bridge_gpio_request;
+	gd32->chip.get		  = gd32_bridge_gpio_get;
+	gd32->chip.set		  = gd32_bridge_gpio_set;
+	gd32->chip.direction_output = gd32_bridge_gpio_dir_out;
+	gd32->chip.direction_input  = gd32_bridge_gpio_dir_in;
+	gd32->chip.get_direction    = gd32_bridge_gpio_get_dir;
+	gd32->chip.can_sleep	  = true;
+
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(&client->dev, gd32_bridge_cancel_replay,
+					gd32);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * Nothing to replay yet: output_mask starts at 0, so the periodic
+	 * work is not queued here. gd32_bridge_gpio_do_set() (re)kicks it
+	 * the first time any line is written.
+	 */
+
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(&client->dev, gd32_bridge_cancel_resolve,
+					gd32);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * Kick off the independent lines-18/19 resolver now if the
+	 * best-effort handshake above didn't already land -- see
+	 * gd32_bridge_resolve_work() and the file-header comment. Not
+	 * needed (and not started) if the handshake already resolved
+	 * wifi_bt_reg_on_status to 0 or -ENODEV.
+	 */
+	if (gd32->wifi_bt_reg_on_status == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+		queue_delayed_work(system_freezable_power_efficient_wq,
+				   &gd32->resolve_work,
+				   msecs_to_jiffies(GD32_RESOLVE_POLL_PERIOD_MS));
+
+	i2c_set_clientdata(client, gd32);
+	return devm_gpiochip_add_data(&client->dev, &gd32->chip, gd32);
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id gd32_bridge_gpio_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "alplab,gd32-bridge-gpio" },
+	{ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, gd32_bridge_gpio_of_match);
+
+static struct i2c_driver gd32_bridge_gpio_driver = {
+	.driver = {
+		.name = "gpio-gd32-bridge",
+		.of_match_table = gd32_bridge_gpio_of_match,
+	},
+	.probe_new = gd32_bridge_gpio_probe,
+};
+
+/*
+ * Not module_i2c_driver(): gd32_bridge_kick_driver (see the file-header
+ * comment and gd32_bridge_kick_deferred_probe()) needs registering
+ * alongside the i2c driver, so both are done explicitly here.
+ */
+static int __init gd32_bridge_gpio_module_init(void)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = platform_driver_register(&gd32_bridge_kick_driver);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = i2c_add_driver(&gd32_bridge_gpio_driver);
+	if (ret)
+		platform_driver_unregister(&gd32_bridge_kick_driver);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+module_init(gd32_bridge_gpio_module_init);
+
+static void __exit gd32_bridge_gpio_module_exit(void)
+{
+	i2c_del_driver(&gd32_bridge_gpio_driver);
+	platform_driver_unregister(&gd32_bridge_kick_driver);
+}
+module_exit(gd32_bridge_gpio_module_exit);
+
+MODULE_AUTHOR("Alp Lab AB");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("GPIO driver for the Alp Lab GD32 IO-MCU bridge");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
